{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2223 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "614bae9bd2e1794c8e9f4912d665e9154467b8faa82bb5cfc4a079660a1b9278" :edb/db-before-t 2222 :edb/db-after-t 2223 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535535 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:00:51.462Z" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166417 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}$.\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535535 false] [17592186166417 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/assets/35bcfa2242051bed034ac3343c1a6d67f94916d4267030f726e2bd27cbbe02f1.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166417 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨z,-y,-x⟩)/(\\sqrt{(z)^{2} + (-y)^{2} + (-x)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{z^{2} + y^{2} + x^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot ⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (xz - 2yz - x(z - y))/(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{xy - 2yz}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{x y - 2 y z}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 false] [17592186166417 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{(z)^{2}+(-y)^{2}+(-x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz-2yz-x(z-y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166418 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$." 13194139535535 false] [17592186166418 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166418 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x\\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0\\leq y\\leq 1\n$$\n\nthe downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨z, z, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&=-4xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)(-4xy)dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-4xy)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(-4xy)dxdy \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{1}2ydy∫_{0}^{1}2xdx \\\\\n&= - [y^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 false] [17592186166418 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166419 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r_x' \\times \\mathbf r_y' \\mid \\mid =3$.*" 13194139535535 false] [17592186166419 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||\\mathbf r_x \\times \\mathbf r_y|| =3.$*" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166419 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface\n\n$$\nz = 2x+2y\n$$\n\ncan be written using the dot product as\n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S$, we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩)/(\\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 1^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nz=2x+2y\n$$\n\non $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨y, 0, z⟩ \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨y, 0, 2x + 2y⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-2y + 0x + 2x + 2y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{2}{3}xdS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot \\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid \\mid dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2xdA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}2xdxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}dy \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2}2xdx \\\\\n&= [y]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 false] [17592186166419 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,0,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2y+0x+2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186166812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354148 13194139535535 true] [17592186166812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354155 13194139535535 true] [17592186166812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354162 13194139535535 true] [17592186166812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354169 13194139535535 true] [17592186166813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354134 13194139535535 true] [17592186166813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354204 13194139535535 true] [17592186166813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354211 13194139535535 true] [17592186166813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354218 13194139535535 true] [17592186166813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354225 13194139535535 true] [17592186166814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354141 13194139535535 true] [17592186166814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354176 13194139535535 true] [17592186166814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354183 13194139535535 true] [17592186166814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354190 13194139535535 true] [17592186166814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354197 13194139535535 true] [17592186167064 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535535 true] [17592186167064 1100 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535535 true] [17592186167064 1100 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $n^(~)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩, n^(~)(x, y, z) = ⟨2y,-z, 2x⟩, n = (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖)$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(S)\\frac{AAAAAAA}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535535 false] [17592186167064 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354134 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354135 13194139535535 true] [17592186354134 1089 "q-161192" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354134 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535535 true] [17592186354134 1100 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354134 1113 #uuid "0fb338bb-c8d9-50c5-addd-465e82c29978" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354134 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for  $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,4x,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354136 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354137 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354138 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354139 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354140 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1041 "selection" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354140 13194139535535 true] [17592186354135 1110 #uuid "3e9d31df-64fd-5993-9d88-c8494001bc99" 13194139535535 true] [17592186354136 1034 "